    description: "PR1 of the four-PR closure sequence authorised by the owner 2026-08-23\n(\"declare the blindness, do those 4 PRs\" -- full ruling on INV-polad).\n\nTHE GAP, measured, not inferred. INV-fibis's untyped-receiver disclosure is\ngated on `meta.call_construct == \"method\"` (verify_claims.untyped_receiver_sites).\nOne fixture per language, each calling a sink from that language's OWN\nio_primitives catalogue on a receiver the analyzer cannot type\n(~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/disclosure_parity_08232026/):\n\n  lang        call edges  external  construct=method   verdict\n  python               1         1                 1   disclosure CAN fire\n  go                   1         1                 1   disclosure CAN fire\n  java                 2         2                 0   edge, NO stamp -> INERT\n  scala                1         1                 0   edge, NO stamp -> INERT\n  swift                1         1                 0   edge, NO stamp -> INERT\n  objc                 1         1                 0   edge, NO stamp -> INERT\n  rust                 2         2                 0   edge, NO stamp -> INERT\n  kotlin               0         0                 0   NO EDGE (PR2's problem)\n  javascript           0         0                 0   NO EDGE (PR2's problem)\n\nFIVE LANGUAGES ALREADY EMIT THE EDGE AND ONLY LACK THE META KEY. That is 554\nof the 954 method-kind sinks across the nine catalogued languages, recoverable\nby stamping a key the consumer already reads. java/scala/swift/objc/rust each\nstamp `call_construct` on their RESOLVED paths (rust does it in three places)\nand omit it on the UNRESOLVED-EXTERNAL path -- which is precisely the\npopulation the disclosure exists for.\n\nSCOPE OF THIS PR:\n  1. Stamp `call_construct: \"method\"` on the unresolved-external\n     instance-method call path in java, scala, swift, objc, rust.\n  2. A REGISTRY-DRIVEN PARITY GATE. For every language whose io_primitives\n     catalogue declares any method-kind sink, a fixture with an untypable\n     receiver must produce a caveat rather than a bare `confirmed`. A language\n     with method-kind sinks and no fixture FAILS the gate.\n\nTHE GATE IS THE POINT, not the five stamps. Without it, language number ten\narrives with method sinks, no stamp, and reaches a silent clean verdict while\nevery test stays green -- which is how this became an 82% blind spot without\nanyone noticing. LIVE.md rule 7: a predicate is inert until its call sites\npass it, and the site that RUNS may not be the one that READS it.\n\nDONE WHEN: the parity table above shows \"disclosure CAN fire\" for all seven\nedge-emitting languages, the two no-edge languages are the only exceptions and\nare handed to PR2, and the gate fails on a deliberately un-fixtured language.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: kotlin / javascript (no edge to stamp -- PR2); the rust denylist\nthat removes the call site before any stamp could apply (PR3); which analyzer\nproduced the edge set (PR4/WI-lagod)."  # e83e
    fields: {}  # e83e
- op: discuss  # 8256
  at: '2026-08-24T04:01:21Z'  # 8256
  by: agent  # 8256
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8256
  clock: 2  # 8256
  nonce: '8256'  # 8256
  message: "CORRECTION TO MY OWN MEASUREMENT, before it hardens into a fact anyone builds\non. The 82% figure in the ruling entry above is WRONG. The real figure is 55%,\nand five-languages-need-stamps is really THREE.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. My first fixtures for scala and swift used a CAST receiver\n(f.asInstanceOf[java.io.File].createNewFile(), (fm as! FileManager)\n.createFile(...)). A cast expression takes a different, BARE-CALL branch in\nboth analyzers. The plain `receiver.method()` shape -- the one real code uses\n-- is ALREADY STAMPED CORRECTLY in scala and swift. Re-measured on the plain\nshape:\n\n    python  FIRES     java    INERT (edge emitted, no stamp)\n    go      FIRES     objc    INERT\n    scala   FIRES     rust    INERT\n    swift   FIRES     kotlin  NO EDGE      javascript  NO EDGE\n\n    954 method-kind sinks total\n    295 inert but fixable by a stamp : java 139, objc 122, rust 34\n    232 no edge at all (WI-nasuf)    : kotlin 181, javascript 51\n    527 blind in total = 55%, not 82%\n    427 already firing               : scala 162, python 143, swift 97, go 25\n\nThis is LIVE.md rule 12 in its exact filed form -- a one-arm test of the WRONG\nARM generalises to a falsehood -- and it was caught by instrumenting which\nemit site actually fired rather than by reading the analyzers.\n\nNOTHING ABOUT THE RULING CHANGES. The gap is smaller and the work is smaller;\nthe shape is identical, and 55% of catalogued method sinks reaching a silent\nclean verdict is not a defensible position either.\n\nPR1 IS DONE AND MEASURED AT THREE LAYERS.\n  unit     : the registry-driven parity gate, 9 tests green, and it is driven\n             from the shipped io_primitives catalogues so a language arriving\n             later with method-kind sinks and no fixture FAILS instead of\n             going silently blind.\n  analyzer : java / objc / rust now stamp call_construct on the\n             unresolved-external method path. java's stamp moved OUT of the\n             typed-receiver branch (where it meant \"a method call whose\n             receiver I typed\") to the one place every receiver-bearing call\n             passes; objc stamps unconditionally because a message send is a\n             method call by construction; rust stamps on is_method_call.\n  published: java went from a BARE `confirmed` (rc 0) to\n             `confirmed_with_caveats` (rc 3) -- \"At 2 of 2 method call site(s)\n             the receiver's type could not be determined ... distinct\n             method(s): u.mkdirs, xs.size\". The CONTROL ARM (same claim, a\n             TYPED `java.io.File` receiver) stays `violated` rc 1, so this is\n             not a blanket downgrade. objc likewise moved to\n             confirmed_with_caveats rc 3.\n\nRECALL COST: none, and gate_named_entry is where you can see why -- with no\nmodule hint it refuses a method-kind hit EITHER WAY, through the\n`call_construct == \"method\"` arm with the stamp and through the `non_method`\nfilter without it. The stamp only buys the ability to SAY SO.\n\nTWO RESIDUALS FOUND AT LAYER 3, filed on the PR1 item rather than fixed here:\njava's callee name carries the receiver prefix (`u.mkdirs`), so only the\nUNSCOPED `unknown_receiver_scope` caveat fires and the boundary-scoped\n`untyped_receiver` one cannot match the catalogue's `mkdirs`; and objc's\ncaveat renders its method name as empty (\"distinct method(s): .\") even though\nparts[3] of the id is `createFileAtPath`, so the cause is elsewhere and is NOT\nyet located. rust's layer-3 arm still returns `inconclusive` for an unrelated\nreason -- WI-lutuh, rust.yaml declaring zero module_completeness -- so its\nstamp is verified at the unit layer only."  # 8256
